(2) migrations/000183_snapshots_si.up.sql location_snapshots schema (lines 343-351)

  CREATE TABLE location_snapshots (
    vehicle_id  BIGINT      NOT NULL,
    ts          TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
    place       TEXT,
    country     TEXT,
    region      TEXT,
    geocoded_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
    PRIMARY KEY (vehicle_id, ts)
  );

  Per migration comment (lines 337-342) the table is populated by the
  geocoding worker on its own write path — not by telemetry atomics
  flowing through normalize.Pipeline. routing.yaml accordingly carries
  no entries today; this writer is authored anyway per Decision #3 so
  the router constructor in 0050's MQTT cutover can wire one writer
  per Destination const without a "no writer for destination
  location_snapshot" panic the moment a future routing.yaml entry
  adds a location_snapshot route.

(3) Schema column-type inventory (forward compatibility)

  TEXT (3 cols, codec emits string):
    place, country, region

  TIMESTAMPTZ (1 col, codec emits time.Time after EmittedAt round-trip):
    geocoded_at

  ⇒ The TEXT columns sit inside snapshotWriter's bindSnapshotValue
    happy path (snapshot_base.go lines 194-209 accept string).
  ⇒ The TIMESTAMPTZ column does NOT — bindSnapshotValue rejects
    time.Time. If a future routing entry maps a Field to geocoded_at
    the writer must adopt the hybrid wrapper pattern from
    tire_pressure_writer.go (which dispatches TIMESTAMPTZ-bound
    fields to an in-file writeTimestamp helper). This caveat is
    documented in location_writer.go's locationColumnByField godoc.

  Today's empty map keeps the writer inside the pure snapshotWriter
  composition because there is no field to dispatch.

(4) Predecessor-pattern provenance

  - File shape: NewLocationWriter(*pgxpool.Pool) router.Writer
    mirrors NewSafetyWriter byte-for-byte except for table name +
    column map + identifier names — locked by Decision #1+#2.
  - Test shape: 4 top-level tests (Coverage / EmptyMapConstructs /
